    {
      "id": "library/10665-331525/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Chapter 1. Introduction and approach",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-chapter-1-introduction-and-approach.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Introduction and approach\nIntroduction\nAbout one fourth of the world’s population is estimated to be infected with M. tuberculosis (1,2). The \nrisk of TB disease after infection depends on several factors, the most important being weakened \nimmunological status (3). The vast majority of infected individuals show no signs or symptoms of TB \nand are not infectious, although they have an increased risk of progressing to TB disease and becoming \ninfectious. On average, about 5–10% of those infected will develop TB disease over the course of their \nlives, most of them within the first five years af",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces the TB preventive treatment handbook, explaining that about a quarter of people worldwide are infected with M. tuberculosis and that people with HIV and contacts of TB patients are at highest risk. It places programmatic management of preventive treatment within the End TB Strategy and the 2018 UN high-level meeting targets, noting low coverage so far. It describes the handbook's purpose, intended users and approach of guiding countries through decision points. A box summarises WHO recommendations and urges governments and donors to expand access.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
