    {
      "id": "library/10665-331525/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Chapter 2. Identification of populations for TB preventive treatment",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 31,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-chapter-2-identification-of-populations-for-tb-p.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "populations for TB preventive \ntreatment\nDecision point on target populations for systematic \ntesting and TPT\nWhich of the at-risk populations are priority targets for the programmatic scale-up of TPT, based on \nyour country context?\nThe WHO guidelines on TPT recommends target populations fulfilling one or more of the following \ncriteria for PMTPT (12):\n•\t High prevalence of TB infections\n•\t High risk of progression to TB disease\n•\t High incidence of TB disease compared to the general population, indicating high TB transmission \nsetting\n•\t Benefits of TPT outweighing potential risk of acquirin",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses which at-risk populations countries should prioritise for systematic testing and TB preventive treatment, based on infection prevalence, progression risk and transmission. It sets out the rationale and WHO recommendations for people living with HIV, including children and infants, household contacts of all ages, and clinical risk groups such as patients on dialysis or anti-TNF therapy. It also covers prisoners, health workers, migrants and other groups that may be considered case by case. The chapter ends with implementation considerations for contact investigation and a call to invest in it.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-331525/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Chapter 3. Ruling out TB disease before TB preventive treatment",
      "pageStart": 31,
      "pageEnd": 39,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-chapter-3-ruling-out-tb-disease-before-tb-preven.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "before TB preventive treatment\nDecision point on how to exclude TB disease before \noffering TPT\nHow can TB disease be reliably ruled out among target populations identified for TPT?\nOffering TPT to someone who has TB disease can delay the resolution of disease and favour the \nemergence of drug resistance. Thus, excluding TB disease before initiating TPT is one of the critical \nsteps in the preventive TB care pathway. This chapter covers WHO recommendations as well as key \npolicy and implementation considerations in the development of national algorithms to rule out TB \ndisease, mindful of the",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how to rule out TB disease before starting preventive treatment, since treating active disease this way risks delays and drug resistance. It reviews the sensitivity of symptom screening in different groups and the added value of chest radiography, citing related WHO recommendations. It discusses algorithms combining symptoms, imaging and tests for people with HIV, contacts and other risk groups. It ends with practical steps such as expanding rapid diagnostics, specimen transport and standard operating procedures.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-331525/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Chapter 4. Testing for TB infection",
      "pageStart": 39,
      "pageEnd": 47,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-chapter-4-testing-for-tb-infection.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "infection\nDecision point on role of TST or IGRA \nWhat is the role of testing for TB infection before starting TPT among target populations prioritized \nby the ministry of health?\nJust as excluding TB disease is a critical step before starting TPT, confirming TB infection before starting \nTPT may increase the certainty that individuals targeted for TPT would benefit from it. However, there \nis no gold standard test to diagnose TB infection. Currently available tests are indirect and require the \nperson to mount an immune response in order to work properly. A positive test result by either metho",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses the role of the tuberculin skin test and interferon-gamma release assays before preventive treatment, stressing there is no gold standard for TB infection. It explains how each test works, their limitations and false negatives, and when testing need not delay treatment, such as for people with HIV and young child contacts. It covers test choice, logistics and programme considerations, and includes an algorithm flowchart for deciding on preventive treatment. It ends with a call to invest in shorter regimens.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-331525/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Chapter 5. TB preventive treatment",
      "pageStart": 47,
      "pageEnd": 67,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-chapter-5-tb-preventive-treatment.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "treatment\nDecision point on choice of TB preventive treatment \nWhich of the WHO recommended TPT regimens should be used for different individuals in the country?\nTPT regimen recommendation and availability\nTPT broadly falls into two categories: (i) isoniazid monotherapy for six or nine months, or (ii) rifamycin-\nbased shorter preventive treatment, on the assumption that the infecting strain is susceptible to these \nmedicines. Isoniazid preventive treatment (IPT) for six months has been the most widely used regimen \nunder programmatic conditions and has emerged as a standard for TPT for both ad",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Covers the choice among WHO-recommended TB preventive treatment regimens, from six or nine months of isoniazid to shorter rifamycin-based options such as 3HP, 3HR, 1HP and 4R. It reviews evidence on efficacy and completion rates and explains the 2020 changes to recommended durations. It also addresses dosing, regimens for contacts of drug-resistant TB, pregnancy, children and drug interactions with antiretrovirals and other medicines. The chapter closes with guidance on people who use drugs and a call to raise demand for preventive treatment.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-331525/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Chapter 6. Safety, management of adverse reactions and other drug-related issues in TB preventive treatment",
      "pageStart": 67,
      "pageEnd": 83,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-chapter-6-safety-management-of-adverse-reactions.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "of adverse reactions and \nother drug-related issues in \nTB preventive treatment \nWHO has for long recommended the use of TPT for populations at risk of TB, particularly PLHIV \nand child household contacts of TB patients. However programmatic scale-up of TPT has remained \nlimited in most high TB and HIV burden countries due to other competing priorities. Concerns about \nthe efficacy and safety of TPT and interactions with other medicines, particularly ARVs, may also be \nbarriers to programmatic scale-up. Other frequently raised questions are about the potential for TPT \nto increase TB drug-resi",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Addresses safety concerns that hinder scale-up of TB preventive treatment, including adverse drug reactions, monitoring and counselling. It tabulates known adverse events for isoniazid and rifamycins and details hepatotoxicity risks and their management. Further sections discuss interactions with other medicines, adherence, the durability of protection and fears that preventive treatment fosters drug resistance. It cites trial data finding no significant rise in rifampicin resistance and calls for patient support to ensure completion.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-331525/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Chapter 7. Adherence to TB preventive treatment",
      "pageStart": 83,
      "pageEnd": 93,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-chapter-7-adherence-to-tb-preventive-treatment.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "preventive treatment \nDecision point on strategies to promote adherence \nto TPT\nWhat strategies should national programmes adopt to ensure adherence to the prescribed regimen \nby all individuals started on TPT?\nAdherence to treatment is a complex behaviour that is influenced by many factors such as personal \nmotivation, beliefs about health, risks and benefits from treatment, comorbidities, competing demands \nthat conflict with the taking of medicine, family environment, complexity of the drug regimen, drug \ntoxicity, trust and relationship with the health provider (58). Effective person-centr",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Chapter 7 of a WHO operational handbook on tuberculosis preventive treatment (TPT) addresses how national programmes can help people complete their prescribed regimen. It explains that adherence depends on many personal, social and treatment-related factors and lists person-centred measures such as confidential counselling, education in the person's language, involving families and giving clear advice on side-effects. It also covers warning people that TB disease can still develop during TPT, along with options such as reminders, digital adherence technologies, shorter regimens and community support. The chapter closes by urging investment in monitoring and in digital tools for recording and reporting TPT services.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-331525/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Chapter 8. Monitoring and evaluation",
      "pageStart": 93,
      "pageEnd": 103,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-chapter-8-monitoring-and-evaluation.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "evaluation\nDecision point on minimum data for routine \nrecording and reporting\n•\t What data should be routinely captured and reported to the national level for \nprogramme management?\n•\t What is the minimum dataset needed to monitor individual case management and monitor \nprogress across the TPT pathway?\n•\t What tools and methods should be used to facilitate data collection and reporting?\nMonitoring and evaluation (M&E) play an important role in patient care and in assessing national \nprogrammes and global response (Fig. 8.1). Accurate recording and reporting of programmatic \ndata informs manag",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Chapter 8 of the TPT handbook sets out what data should be routinely recorded and reported, the minimum dataset for following people along the TPT cascade, and the tools for collecting it. It explains how monitoring and evaluation serve patient care, national programme management and global accountability, with examples such as counting household contacts evaluated and people completing TPT. Material on the cascade of care tracks numbers targeted, evaluated, eligible, started and completed, and explains how to estimate target populations among people living with HIV and household contacts. The chapter ends with a reminder that programmes must counsel people to make informed choices and protect them from stigma.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
