    {
      "id": "library/10665-331525/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Chapter 9. Ethics and TB preventive treatment",
      "pageStart": 103,
      "pageEnd": 106,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-chapter-9-ethics-and-tb-preventive-treatment.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Chapter 9. Ethics and TB \npreventive treatment\nDecision on ethical issues in TPT implementation\nWhat are ethical considerations and related actions for programme management of TPT?\nImplementation considerations \nIn general, only about 5–10% of people infected with M. tuberculosis will develop TB disease at some \npoint in their lifetime. The risk of TB infection progressing to TB disease is much higher for some \ngroups, such as PLHIV, very young children and people who have just recently acquired infection. \nTreatment always carries some risk of adverse drug reactions, so understanding the indi",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Chapter 9 of the TPT handbook discusses ethical considerations in delivering preventive treatment to people who are not ill or infectious. It stresses weighing individual benefits against harms, limiting testing and treatment to groups at proven risk, and ensuring that taking TPT is a voluntary, informed choice grounded in human rights. It also addresses equity, confidentiality, stigma and the need for adequate communication resources so that individuals and communities can decide autonomously.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-331525/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Annex 1. List of key messages",
      "pageStart": 106,
      "pageEnd": 116,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-annex-1-list-of-key-messages.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Messages for the ministry of health\n•\t National governments have made a commitment at the first ever UN High Level Meeting on TB on \n26th September 2018 towards “preventing tuberculosis for those most at risk of falling ill through the \nrapid scaling up of access to testing for tuberculosis infection, according to the domestic situation, and \nthe provision of preventive treatment, with a focus on high-burden countries, so that at least 30 million \npeople, including 4 million children under 5 years of age, 20 million other household contacts of people \naffected by tuberculosis, and 6 million pe",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Annex 1 of the TPT handbook lists key messages for ministries of health and for health care workers. Messages for ministries recall commitments made at the 2018 UN High-Level Meeting on TB and the End TB Strategy targets, and present TPT as an effective intervention that substantially lowers the risk of TB disease. Messages for health workers cover shorter rifapentine- or rifampicin-based regimens, which are reported to be easier to complete, plus counselling before treatment, ruling out TB disease and managing adverse events. Notes point to funding sources and to the Global Drug Facility for medicines.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
