      "content": "asked questions on TST\nWhat is tuberculin skin testing (139)?\nTuberculin skin test (TST), first introduced in 1908, remains the most commonly used test for TB \ninfection in the world due to its low cost and feasibility to implement in low resource settings. There \nis no need for complex laboratory equipment or procedures or trained technicians, although training \nis required for proper intradermal administration of the tuberculin purified protein derivative (PPD). \nIntradermal injection of tuberculin causes delayed hypersensitivity reaction if a person has TB infection. \nThe T-cells in such in",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Annex 5 answers frequently asked questions about the tuberculin skin test (TST) for TB infection. It explains how the test works, the standard tuberculin doses and intradermal injection technique, and how to read and measure the induration, including the ballpoint pen method. It also covers interpreting results, causes of false-positive and false-negative readings, and the limited effect of BCG vaccination given at birth.",
